Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Severe dermatomal/distal allodynia, Preceding systemic infection history, Distal sensory loss

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-020
Reason for Visit: Excruciating, burning pain across the chest wall preventing sleep and restricting breathing.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted severe, unremitting burning pain wrapping around the right thorax, causing shallow breathing and severe distress. Urgent assessment is required to definitively rule out acute cardiopulmonary pathology (e.g., pulmonary embolism, acute coronary syndrome) and provide immediate neuropathic analgesia before regular clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My chest is on fire. It hurts so much I can't even wear a shirt, and I can't take a deep breath because the skin hurts too much to stretch."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports persistent burning, lancinating, and deep aching neuropathic pain strictly localized to the right T6 dermatome. The pain began acutely 48 hours ago. The patient exhibits severe mechanical allodynia and profound hyperesthesia triggered by normally innocuous stimuli, such as clothing resting on the skin. There is a marked exacerbation of neuropathic pain specifically during the night, resulting in severe sleep disruption. The patient has a distinct clinical history of a preceding herpes zoster (shingles) eruption in the exact same dermatome 6 weeks ago, which has since healed. The patient reports severe dermatomal allodynia and distal sensory loss in the affected area.
Functional Status: Independent, but severely restricted by pain; currently sitting completely shirtless to avoid tactile stimulation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Sleep deprivation leading to exhaustion; shallow breathing risking atelectasis.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/90 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 bpm (shallow) | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Anxious, guarding the right side of the chest, taking intentionally shallow breaths.
Physical Exam: Inspection of the right thorax reveals healed, mildly hyperpigmented macular scarring in a distinct T6 dermatomal distribution (trophic skin changes). No active vesicular lesions. There is exquisite mechanical allodynia to light cotton touch precisely over the T6 dermatome on the right, wrapping from the spine to the sternum. Sensation to pinprick is subjectively altered (hyperalgesia) in the same distribution. Cardiopulmonary examination reveals clear lung fields bilaterally and normal heart sounds, completely lacking signs of pleurisy, consolidation, or ischemia. No palpable cutaneous purpura or vasculitic ulcers.
Environmental Observations: House is excessively warm; patient using a fan to cool the skin.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol and Ibuprofen providing zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Herpetic Neuralgia (PHN) - Status: Acute Pain Exacerbation
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is strictly dermatomal, burning/electrical rather than crushing/pressure, heavily provoked by cutaneous touch, and normal cardiopulmonary exam.

Pulmonary Embolism / Pleurisy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear lungs, normal SpO2, and pain is explicitly triggered by superficial skin contact (allodynia) rather than deep inspiration itself.

Thoracic Radiculopathy (structural) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear temporal relationship to a recent, anatomically identical herpes zoster eruption.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering from excruciating post-herpetic neuralgia, a severe, localized neuropathic pain syndrome. The intensity of the thoracic pain prompted an after-hours call due to fears of a heart attack and severe respiratory splinting. Delaying assessment would subject the patient to agonizing, unremitting pain, profound insomnia, and a risk of basal atelectasis due to continuous shallow breathing. Urgent clinical examination successfully ruled out life-threatening cardiopulmonary emergencies, definitively diagnosing a post-infectious neuropathic syndrome. This permitted the immediate initiation of targeted, membrane-stabilizing neuropathic analgesia and topical treatments, strictly fulfilling MBS criteria for an urgent, necessary intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Pregabalin 75mg orally STAT, then 75mg at night. Prescribed topical 5% Lidocaine patches to be applied strictly to the intact, painful skin for 12 hours on, 12 hours off.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By applying the Lidocaine patch directly over the painful area, the patient actively numbs the hyperactive nerve endings locally, instantly allowing them to tolerate clothing and deep breaths.
Step 2: By taking deep, slow, and deliberate breaths every hour, the patient fully expands their lungs, completely preventing the development of a secondary chest infection caused by shallow pain-breathing.
Step 3: By wearing loose, soft, 100% cotton clothing instead of tight synthetic fabrics, the patient radically reduces mechanical friction on the hyper-sensitive skin, preventing continuous pain flare-ups.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to titrate Pregabalin and monitor pain control.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Attend the Emergency Department if the pain suddenly changes character to a deep, crushing chest pressure, radiates to the left arm or jaw, or if you become severely short of breath at rest.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Post-herpetic neuralgia can be a chronic condition; complete pain resolution takes time and medication titration.
Clinical Course: The topical patches provide immediate, localized relief. The oral nerve medication will gradually build up in your system over the next 1-2 weeks to calm the damaged nerve root.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for review if the pain completely fails to improve after 48 hours on the new medications, or if the skin breaks down or develops a new rash under the patches.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The shingles virus damaged the sensory nerve in your chest wall. Even though the rash is gone, the nerve is misfiring, sending false, severe pain signals to your brain when anything touches the skin. Standard painkillers do not work. The patch numbs the skin, and the oral medication calms the misfiring nerve.
Actionable Steps: Apply the Lidocaine patch now. Take the Pregabalin 2 hours before bed. Practice deep breathing exercises hourly.

